# Global Codex Working Agreements

These instructions define workstation-wide defaults for Codex. Repository-level
instructions and task contracts may refine or override them.

## Working model

- Treat the repository as the durable source of truth for project state,
  decisions, requirements, tasks, and engineering evidence.
- Do not assume that ChatGPT, another Codex session, another model, or another
  agent has access to the current conversation. Persist important information
  in repository artifacts when the project workflow requires it.
- When a repository defines its own `AGENTS.md`, AI workflow, active task,
  architecture decisions, or canonical commands, read and follow them before
  substantive work.

## Engineering behavior

- Understand the existing implementation and constraints before editing.
- Prefer the smallest coherent change that satisfies the requested outcome.
- Do not broaden scope, redesign architecture, add production dependencies,
  or change public contracts without clear justification and authorization.
- Prefer repository-defined canonical commands and workflows over ad-hoc
  commands when they exist.
- Establish relevant baseline status before material changes when practical.
- Verify changes with the strongest proportionate deterministic evidence
  available: build, type checks, linters, tests, security checks, runtime
  behavior, or other repository-defined gates.
- Never weaken or remove a quality gate merely to make a change pass.
- Report what was actually verified and clearly identify anything not verified.

## Risk and escalation

- Stop and escalate when discovered work materially exceeds the assigned scope,
  risk, architectural impact, or execution profile.
- Do not perform destructive, irreversible, privileged, or production actions
  without explicit authorization.
- Never expose, commit, log, or reproduce secrets or credentials.

## Documentation and language

- Write durable engineering artifacts in English unless the repository or
  product explicitly requires localized user-facing content.
- Keep durable documentation synchronized when behavior, architecture,
  interfaces, operational procedures, or important decisions change.

## Tools and skills

- Use installed skills when they materially match the task; do not load or
  invoke workflows merely because they are available.
- Prefer current authoritative documentation for APIs, frameworks, tools,
  security-sensitive behavior, and other information likely to change.
